A word about packages, because this is where the advantage of R actually lies. The tidyverse gives you one consistent grammar for reading data, reshaping it and plotting it, so once you have learned the verbs they work everywhere. Easystats turns model output back into tidy data – coefficients, fit statistics, effect sizes – instead of the printed blob that summary gives you. Modelsummary builds publication-ready regression tables straight from fitted model objects, so your table cannot disagree with your model. And marginaleffects computes predictions, contrasts and marginal effects from almost any model you can fit, which is how you get from a coefficient to a statement in the units that your reader thinks in. The tasks also use fixest for fast fixed effects – that is the estimator behind our baseline – and dagitty and ggdag for the causal graphs. The check script in your workspace reports whether those three are installed.
