:::

::: notes
And the last group: where things go so they are still there in ten years. OSF is free, holds projects and files and registrations, and the whole public record of our case is kept there – you will be reading it later. Zenodo is run by CERN, gives you a DOI, and will take a GitHub release directly, which makes archiving a one-click habit. Dataverse is the institutional data-archive world with proper metadata and curation. And then the domain archives – GESIS, the UK Data Service, ICPSR – which is where licensed survey microdata is held, including the Eurobarometer data behind our paper. A repository is not an archive, although people often treat it as one. A GitHub repository can have its history rewritten, be renamed or be deleted tomorrow, and the link in your paper dies. An archived deposit has a DOI, a fixed version and an institution committed to serving it. Serious work usually needs both.
:::

