Licensing gets skipped and then bites. Four rows. For code, MIT says use it however you like, GPL says use it and share your changes back. For data, CC-zero puts it in the public domain and CC-BY asks for attribution. For text and figures and slides, CC-BY or CC-BY with a non-commercial clause. And the fourth row is the one that constrains this course: when the data are not yours, their terms govern. The Eurobarometer microdata behind our paper is GESIS-licensed – I am not allowed to redistribute it, which is why you will be working on an aggregate I built and am allowed to share, and it is also the reason why the first SCORE attempt to reproduce this paper failed, which we come back to when we reach the paper itself. And the line at the bottom: leaving a repository without a licence does not make it free to use. Legally it means all rights reserved, so nobody may reuse it at all.
