## What the curve leaves out {.smaller}

::: {.small}
- **The options left off, and the specifications that nobody ran.** The audit moved pooled OLS, random effects, the restricted samples and the weights off the curve, and nobody ran person-level models or a lagged predictor. The curve looks just as complete either way
- **The dependence.** All 864 use the same 270 country-years, so they share their noise, and the width of the cloud cannot be read as 864 independent results
- **Estimand drift.** Some of these specifications answer subtly different questions. The chart puts them on one axis; that does not make them commensurable
- **The data-building choices.** Everything upstream of the panel – how the scales were coded, which waves were pooled – is fixed for everyone here
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
A curve is a **map of the menu you wrote**, and it cannot show whether that menu was the right one.
:::

::: notes
Four things are missing from the specification curve, and a chart as tidy as ours makes it easy to think it is complete. First, what is not on it. Some options were left off on purpose, because the audit judged that they answer another question or answer this one less well, and the decision map gives the reason for each. Others were never run by anyone: person-level models, a lagged predictor. The curve looks just as complete either way, which is what makes this easy to miss. Second, the dependence: all eight hundred and sixty-four of these use the same two hundred and seventy country-years, so they share their noise, and the width of that cloud cannot be read as eight hundred and sixty-four independent results. Third, estimand drift: some of these answer subtly different questions, and putting them on one axis does not make them commensurable. And fourth, everything upstream of the panel – how the scales were coded, which waves were pooled – is fixed for everyone in this class. A curve is a map of the menu you wrote, and it cannot show whether the menu was right.
:::

## Description and inference {#two-jobs-one-curve}

::: {.none}
**Description** – map how the estimate moves across every defensible choice. *(What our curve does.)*

::: {.fragment}
**Inference** – ask whether the curve *as a whole* is surprising if there were no effect at all. *(A different, harder job.)*
:::

::: {.fragment}
The two are detachable, and most published curves stop at description.
:::
:::

::: {.src}
@SteegenEtAl2016IncreasingTransparencyMultiverse; @SimonsohnEtAl2020SpecificationCurveAnalysis
:::

::: notes
A curve like ours does two different jobs. The first is description, showing how the estimate moves as the choices move. That is what we built, and it is useful on its own. The second is inference: asking whether the curve, taken as a whole, is more extreme than you would expect if there were no effect at all. Those are different questions with different machinery, and they detach cleanly – Multi100 did the description without the inference, and so far we have done the same. The next three slides are about what the second job takes.
:::

## Dependence among the estimates {#estimates-are-not-840-tests}

:::: {.none}
::: {.fragment .fadeInUp}
Every specification re-uses the **same 270 country-years**. The estimates are deeply dependent.
:::

::: {.fragment .fadeInUp}
'70.9% below zero with bootstrap p < 0.05' **describes** the curve. It is not a p-value for the claim. The bootstrap allows for the years of one country resembling each other within each fit; it does not make the fits independent of one another.
:::

::: {.fragment .fadeInUp}
Vote-counting across dependent specifications has no null distribution, so a tally of significant results is not evidence for the claim on its own.
:::
::::

::: notes
Seventy-one per cent of our specifications lie below zero with a bootstrap p under point zero five, and it is very tempting to read that as evidence strength: "most tests pass, so the claim holds". The bootstrap already allows for the years of one country resembling each other inside each fit. But all eight hundred and sixty-four specifications use the same two hundred and seventy country-years. They share the data, so they share the noise: if sampling luck pushed a handful of country-years in the direction of the claim, it pushed hundreds of specifications the same way at once. The percentage significant is a description of the curve, not a test of the claim. To test the claim at curve level you need to know what the whole curve would look like if there were nothing to find, and that null has to be constructed.
:::

## The permutation design {#building-the-nothing-the-permutation-design}

::: {.small}
- Break the one link that the claim needs: **shuffle each country's unemployment series in time** (jointly with growth), keeping everything else intact
- Refit **every specification on the curve** on each shuffled dataset · repeat **B = 500** times
- Compare the observed curve to the 500 null curves: is ours more extreme than chance produces?
:::

::: {.src}
The design is worked through in your inference reading; permutation inference after @PhipsonSmyth2010PermutationPValues.
:::

::: notes
The claim says that a country's unemployment moves its EU framing over time. To build the null, take each country and shuffle its unemployment series in time – together with growth, so the macro side stays internally coherent – while leaving the framing data untouched. In a shuffled world the alignment between unemployment and framing is broken by construction: whatever curve you get is what pure chance produces on these data with these specifications. Do that five hundred times, refit the entire curve each time, and you have five hundred null curves to compare ours with. If our curve falls in the far tail of those null curves, the curve as a whole is evidence. If it falls comfortably among them, the description stands but the inference does not.
:::

## Reading a curve-level test {#how-to-read-a-curve-level-test .smaller}

::: {.small}
- With B = 500, the smallest reportable p is **2/(B+1) ≈ 0.004**, a floor set by the number of draws, not a zero
- Near p = .05 the Monte-Carlo error is about **±0.01**, so treat p = .04 and p = .06 as the same finding
- [A DESIGN · NOT YET RUN]{.highlight bg-colour="#ffe3b3"} – a curve-level test has to be fully specified before anyone runs it, and this one has not been run; today we read the design only
:::

::: {.fragment}
**Discussion (2 minutes, then chat):** the cosmopolitan scale on the curve has 288 specifications: **94.4% below zero, 55.9% below zero with bootstrap p < 0.05.** Strong evidence? *Against what null?*
:::

::: notes
Two literacy points and then you argue. First: with five hundred draws, the smallest p-value that the test can report is two over five hundred and one – about point zero zero four. That is a floor set by the number of draws, not a zero, and more draws lower it at a real cost in computing time. Second: near the point-oh-five boundary the Monte-Carlo error is around plus or minus point zero one, so a permutation p of point zero four and one of point zero six count as the same finding. The badge says that this test is designed and has not been run, so what you are reading today is a plan whose result nobody yet knows, which is the position that a preregistration puts you in. Now the discussion beat. Take the cosmopolitan scale alone, the outcome that Multi100 instructed: two hundred and eighty-eight specifications on the curve, ninety-four per cent below zero, fifty-six per cent below zero with a bootstrap p under point zero five. Two minutes: is that strong evidence? Against what null? Type your answer. [harvest: the sharp ones will say – we don't know until we know what chance produces on these dependent data; that is the point.]
:::

## What stands without the test

:::: {.none}
::: {.fragment .fadeInUp}
Everything descriptive survives: the share that the **estimator** carries, the placement of the reference results, where the sign changes come from, **your dot**.
:::

::: {.fragment .fadeInUp}
Only one sentence depends on the permutation test: "the curve as a whole is unlikely under no effect".
:::

::: {.fragment .fadeInUp}
Careful reporting says which of the two layers a claim belongs to.
:::
::::

::: notes
Most of what the curve has shown needs no permutation at all. That the estimator accounts for the largest share of the variance – description, stands. Where the six reference results sit once each is converted on its own degrees of freedom – stands. That the sign changes come from pooled OLS and from the samples without the bailout countries – stands. Your own dot and the whole structure of the curve stand as well. The single sentence that has to wait is "this curve as a whole is unlikely if there is no effect". Keeping those two layers apart in your own writing – saying which one your claim belongs to – is what inference literacy means in practice, and it puts you ahead of most published specification curves.
:::

## Critics of the multiverse {#the-multiverse-has-its-critics .smaller}

::: {.small}
- "Robustness is better assessed with a **few thoughtful models** than with billions of regressions" [@Auspurg2025RobustnessBetterAssessed; answering @GanslmeierVlandas2025EstimatingExtentSources] – only *justified* models targeting the *same estimand* belong in one curve. Our curve keeps the **864** specifications that an audit of every option judged justifiable for this claim, and a decomposition by fork does the job that every critic endorses: it locates where the argument must happen
- "**There is only one correct analysis**" once the question is fully specified [@LakensEtAl2026ThereOnlyOne] – your preregistered specification guards against *cherry-picking*, and their deeper point is that preregistering a choice does not yet make it *justified*, which is what the estimand and DAG work were for
- "What's a multiverse **good for** anyway?" – reflection, persuasion, or inference, and each purpose needs a *differently built* universe [@RohrerEtAl2026WhatsMultiverseGood]. Our full menu serves **reflection** and the audited curve moves towards **persuasion**; how much more to read into it is the open debate
:::

::: {.src}
The book-length how-to: @YoungCumberworth2025MultiverseAnalysisComputational; the estimand diagnosis of the red-card study: @AuspurgBruderl2021HasCredibilitySocial.
:::

::: notes
The method you practised is the subject of a live debate, and you can now read that debate for yourself. Katrin Auspurg, who with Josef Brüderl traced the spread in the red-card study to teams answering different questions, answered a 3.6-billion-estimate study with the line at the top of this slide: only thoughtful, justified models belong in a curve. Our own curve is built the way she recommends. Every option of the menu was audited for whether it is a justifiable answer to this claim, the curve keeps eight hundred and sixty-four specifications rather than an enumeration, and decomposing it by fork does the one job that every critic endorses, which is to show where the argument has to happen. Lakens and colleagues say that once the question is fixed, only one analysis implements it correctly. A preregistered specification guards against cherry-picking. Their deeper point, that preregistering a choice does not make it justified, is why the DAG comes before the preregistration. And Rohrer, Hullman and Gelman ask what a multiverse is for: reflection, persuasion, or inference, each needing a differently built set. Our full menu serves reflection, and the audited curve, with a published reason for every option, moves towards persuasion; how much more can be read into it is the open question in the debate you have now joined. [advance]
:::

## The one-correct-analysis argument {#there-is-only-one-correct-analysis .smaller}

::: {.small}
The argument from Lakens and colleagues runs in four steps:

- A research question, **fully specified**, implies a quantity to be estimated
- That quantity, together with the design and the assumptions you are willing to make, implies **one analysis** that estimates it
- So a spread of results is not evidence of legitimate diversity. It is evidence that **most of those specifications are wrong** – they estimate something else
- On this reading, showing a wide curve is closer to an admission than to a robustness check
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Take it back to the red cards. Twenty-nine teams modelled one count of red cards as **linear, logistic or Poisson**. Those are claims about how the outcome was generated, and they cannot all be right – so on this argument most of the spread is error.
:::

::: {.src}
@LakensEtAl2026ThereOnlyOne
:::

::: notes
The strongest objection to the multiverse approach comes from Lakens and colleagues, and I want to give it its full force. They argue that a research question, once fully specified, implies a quantity to be estimated. That quantity, together with your design and the assumptions you are prepared to make, implies one analysis that estimates it. From which it follows that a spread of results is evidence that most of those specifications are wrong, because they estimate something other than what you said you wanted, and not evidence of legitimate analytical diversity at all. And on that reading, publishing a wide specification curve is closer to an admission that you never specified your question than to a robustness check. That is an uncomfortable argument and I think it is largely right.
:::

## Identifying the one correct analysis {#how-would-you-know .smaller}

:::: {.none}
::: {.fragment}
Grant the argument, and suppose that **one correct analysis exists**. Which one is it?
:::

::: {.fragment}
Getting there needs the estimand *and* the identification assumptions *and* agreement about the measurement – and on this claim, reasonable people disagree about all three.
:::

::: {.fragment}
So the curve complements the one-correct-analysis view: it is a **map of the disagreement that remains**, drawn because we cannot yet settle it.
:::

::: {.fragment}
And it points at what to argue about: on this claim, at the **estimator** – whether the comparison removes what all member states shared in a year – and at the **predictor**.
:::
::::

::: notes
So let us grant the argument. Suppose one correct analysis does exist. The next question is which one it is, and how you would know. To get there you need the estimand, and the identification assumptions, and agreement about how the construct is measured – and on this claim reasonable people disagree about all three, as you found for yourselves in the hands-on tasks. So I read the curve as a companion to the one-correct-analysis view rather than a rival to it. It is a map of the disagreement that remains, drawn because we cannot yet settle it. Read that way it is useful, because it shows where the disagreement is. On this claim it is at the estimator, which here is the choice between two questions, change in a country against the common European path or all change within a country, and at the predictor, unemployment or growth. Those are arguments about the claim itself, and they belong in the paper.
:::

## What preregistration settles {.smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**It does settle**

- That your specification was not chosen *because of* the result
- That your rule for what counts as support was fixed before you had a result
- That a reader can see what you intended, and what you changed
:::
:::
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**It does not settle**

- Whether the specification was **any good**
- Whether it estimates the quantity that your question implies
- Whether the assumptions behind it hold

A badly reasoned analysis that is preregistered is still badly reasoned; the preregistration only adds a timestamp
:::
:::
::::

::: {.fragment .small}
That is why the estimand and the DAG came **first** today: preregistration protects a choice, and the causal reasoning is what justifies it.
:::

::: notes
So where does preregistration fit? It settles a specific and limited set of things, and the two columns matter equally. On the left: it settles that your specification was not chosen because of the result it gave, that your rule for what counts as support was fixed before you had a result to bend it around, and that a reader can see both what you intended and what you subsequently changed. Those are real guarantees and no other mechanism provides them. On the right, what it does not settle: whether the specification was any good, whether it estimates the quantity that your question implies, or whether its assumptions hold. A badly reasoned analysis that is preregistered is still badly reasoned, only now with a timestamp on it. That is why we did the estimand and the graph first. Preregistration protects a choice, and the causal reasoning is what justifies it.
:::

