Outside a classroom, an analysis plan is preregistered by filing it in a public registry. Everything you have worked with in this course – the dataset, my analysis script, the codebook – is kept at a permanent public address on the Open Science Framework. OSF does two jobs. It archives materials, which is the only reason you were able to stand in a real analyst's shoes at all. And it registers plans: a registration is a frozen, timestamped snapshot of what you intend to do, filed before you do it, that nobody – including you – can quietly edit afterwards. You do not need an account for any of this today. We are going to read OSF the way a reviewer or a sceptical reader would, starting with the public record of this paper.
:::

